## Summary
This project explores how weaknesses in court administration affect fairness, public trust, and judicial independence in Cameroon. It focuses on court registrars, whose responsibilities in case management, scheduling, and procedural communication significantly influence access to justice. Although laws define their roles, poor institutional support and weak implementation create ethical and operational challenges that can undermine confidence in the judiciary. The study shows that everyday administrative practices shape whether justice is perceived as fair and transparent. It contributes to ongoing debates on judicial reform by highlighting the need for stronger institutional support, clearer professional standards, and improved accountability within African judicial systems.
## Academic abstract
Judicial independence and the proper administration of justice depend not only on judicial decision-making but also on the institutional practices through which courts function on a daily basis. Legal scholarship has largely focused on judges while overlooking the judicial administrative actors who shape court practices. This paper examines how the institutional weaknesses in court administration shape the substance of court registrars and its implications for fairness and legitimacy in Cameroon's judiciary. While statutory frameworks formally define the registrar's status, functions and ethical obligations, persistent gaps in implementation, weak institutional support and unclear hierarchies undermine effective court administration. Drawing on doctrinal analysis and practice-based insights, this paper demonstrates how registrars' management of case files, scheduling, archiving and procedural communication shapes litigants' access to courts and influences whether justice is perceived to be done. It argues that institutional weaknesses affecting registrars generate ethical vulnerabilities that may directly compromise judicial impartiality and independence. By repositioning court registrars at the centre of administrative justice, the paper highlights the need for doctrinal clarity and institutional reform to strengthen the credibility, accountability and future resilience of African judiciaries.
