## Key findings
The project repositions court registrars within judicial reform debates, shifting attention from judges alone to the broader administrative actors who shape justice delivery. It advances scholarship on administrative justice in African judiciaries, offering new socio-legal insight into how everyday institutional practices influence judicial legitimacy and access to justice. The research also speaks to policy and institutional reform, pointing to the need for clearer professional standards, stronger institutional support, and improved accountability mechanisms within court administration — and situates these findings within broader debates on rule of law, ethical governance, and judicial resilience in fragile justice systems.
